per/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042625/state.json" "file_sha256" "1c4576c2fb38c2b66c8cbe2a8cadc680e0a866b52d487a20a08bcf659fd85cb4" "basis" 1561 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042625/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-127069" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the straight line with the canonical equation $\\frac{x-4}{3}=\\frac{y-7}{-1}=\\frac{z+6}{-7}.$ Find the vector equation of the line." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042625/state.json" "file_sha256" "1c4576c2fb38c2b66c8cbe2a8cadc680e0a866b52d487a20a08bcf659fd85cb4" "basis" 1561 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042625/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-127069" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Remember that the Cartesian (canonical) equation of the straight line that passes through the point $P({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ and is parallel to the vector $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y},{v}_{z}⟩$, is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}}=\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}}=\\frac{z-{z}_{0}}{{v}_{z}}=t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nComparing the above to our given equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-4}{3}=\\frac{y-7}{-1}=\\frac{z-(-6)}{-7},\n$$\n\nwe find that our line passes through the point $P(4,7,-6)$ and is parallel to $v=⟨3,-1,-7⟩.$ Therefore, its vector equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =p+tv \\\\ r & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042625/state.json" "file_sha256" "1c4576c2fb38c2b66c8cbe2a8cadc680e0a866b52d487a20a08bcf659fd85cb4" 