 line with the following vector equation:\n\n$$\nr=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042625/state.json" "file_sha256" "1c4576c2fb38c2b66c8cbe2a8cadc680e0a866b52d487a20a08bcf659fd85cb4" "basis" 1561 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042625/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-71048" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Using the usual formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-3}{7}=\\frac{y-(-1)}{-3}=\\frac{z-6}{0}=t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\n$$\n\nor equivalently\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-3}{7}=\\frac{y+1}{-3}=\\frac{z-6}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is problematic because $\\frac{z-6}{0}$ involves division by zero.\n\nHowever, notice that in our line, the $z$ component always maintains the constant value of $z=6:$\n\n$$\nr=[\\begin{aligned}3+7t \\\\ -1-3t \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\n$$\n\nSo, in the Cartesian equation, we remove the $\\frac{z-6}{0}$ part and instead write $z=6$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-3}{7}=\\frac{y+1}{-3},\\,z=6\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042625/state.json" "file_sha256" "1c4576c2fb38c2b66c8cbe2a8cadc680e0a866b52d487a20a08bcf659fd85cb4" "basis" 1561 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042625/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-71048" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042625/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "080ba4d1769da3809cbf2ab4e44f58784d7dd89caeba612e18774fa2b