4}{-5}=\\frac{z-5}{-3}"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062714/state.json" "file_sha256" "511b2fb557b8eb12ca669cf6b53992f7eb13b53e06b7c0fb3fd9d8a7807fc703" "basis" 2007 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062714/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-127466" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{x-1}{8}=\\frac{y-4}{5}=\\frac{z+5}{-3}"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062714/state.json" "file_sha256" "511b2fb557b8eb12ca669cf6b53992f7eb13b53e06b7c0fb3fd9d8a7807fc703" "basis" 2007 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062714/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-127527" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062714/assets/8722b93655e069e2f35147b572c16ed7af408a86f8a88597c79aea8fe59e9c4d.png)\n\nFind a Cartesian equation of the line shown in the figure above." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062714/state.json" "file_sha256" "511b2fb557b8eb12ca669cf6b53992f7eb13b53e06b7c0fb3fd9d8a7807fc703" "basis" 2007 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062714/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-127527" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We know that the line passes through the points $P(2,-1,3)$ and $Q(4,-3,3).$ Now, we need to find a vector parallel to the given line. Let's consider the vector $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ which is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =⟨4,-3,3⟩-⟨2,-1,3⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,-2,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the point $P$ and the vector $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$, we have that the Cartesian (canonical) equation of the given line is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-2}{2}=\\frac{y-(-1)}{-2}=\\frac{z-3}{0}=t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\n$$\n\nor equivalently\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-2}{2}=\\frac{y+1}{-2}=\\frac{z-3}{0}.\n$$\n\nBut the above equation is problematic because $\\frac{z-3}{0}$ involves division by zero.\n\nHowever, in our line, the $z$ component always maintains the constant value of $z=3.$ For example, our two points $P(2,-1,3)$ and $Q(4,-3,3)$ both have $z$-coordinates of $z=3.$\n\nSo, in the Cartesian equation, we remove the $\\frac{z-3}{0}$ part and instead write $z=3,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-2}{2}=\\frac{y+1}{-2},\\,z=3.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062714/state.json" "file_sha256" "511b2fb557b8eb12ca669cf6b53992f7eb13b53e06b7c0fb3fd9d8a7807fc703" "basis" 2007 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062714/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-127527" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062714/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "2d544dca39e67026fca4e2f5913c8e174cb142d54c7a9af77d54db6a8e4ea1aa" "basis" 2007 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062714/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-127527" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062714/state.json" "file_sha256" "511b2fb557b8eb12ca669cf6b53992f7eb13b53e06b7c0fb3fd9d8a7807fc703" "basis" 2007 "v