worked-solution" "value" "First, let's find the corresponding vector equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2-6t \\\\ 1+3t \\\\ -5+2t\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-6t \\\\ 3t \\\\ 2t\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ r & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nThis implies that the straight line passes through the point $P(2,1,-5)$ and is parallel to the vector $v=⟨-6,3,2⟩.$ So, its Cartesian (canonical) equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-2}{-6}=\\frac{y-1}{3}=\\frac{z-(-5)}{2}=t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nSimplifying, we reach\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-2}{-6}=\\frac{y-1}{3}=\\frac{z+5}{2}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083592/state.json" "file_sha256" "79b531f4a33a8e6ecdac0b273aeb259d8b4cc6381e4caa988d49014db4c079a0"} {"question" "q-127405" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083592/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f459ebae02c303ffbdaf1e7e6d5d50f6fa0548822275602509ac63b3920a721f"} {"question" "q-127405" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083592/state.json" "file_sha256" "79b531f4a33a8e6ecdac0b273aeb259d8b4cc6381e4caa988d49014db4c079a0"} {"question" "q-127405" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\f