\\\ -9 \\\\ 11\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083592/state.json" "file_sha256" "79b531f4a33a8e6ecdac0b273aeb259d8b4cc6381e4caa988d49014db4c079a0"} {"question" "q-71039" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Using the usual formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-7}{-3}=\\frac{y-(-9)}{2}=\\frac{z-11}{0}=t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\n$$\n\nor equivalently\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-7}{-3}=\\frac{y+9}{2}=\\frac{z-11}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is problematic because $\\frac{z-11}{0}$ involves division by zero.\n\nHowever, notice that in our line, the $z$ component always maintains the constant value of $z=11:$\n\n$$\nr=[\\begin{aligned}7-3t \\\\ -9+2t \\\\ 11\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\n$$\n\nSo, in the Cartesian equation, we remove the $\\frac{z-11}{0}$ part and instead write $z=11$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-7}{-3}=\\frac{y+9}{2},\\,z=11\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083592/state.json" "file_sha256" "79b531f4a33a8e6ecdac0b273aeb259d8b4cc6381e4caa988d49014db4c079a0"} {"question" "q-71039" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083592/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f459ebae02c303ffbdaf1e7e6d5d50f6fa0548822275602509ac63b3920a721f"} {"question" "q-71039" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083592/state.json" "file_sha256" "79b531f4a33a8e6ecdac0b273aeb259d8b4cc6381e4caa988d49014db4c079a0"} {"question" "q-71039" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{x+3}{7}=\\frac{y-2}{-9},\\,z=0"] ["math" "\\frac{x+7}{-3}=\\frac{y-9}{2},\\,z=-11"] ["math" "\\frac{x-7}{-3}=\\frac{y+9}{2},\\,z=11"] ["math" "\\frac{x-7}{-3}=\\frac{y+9}{2}=z-11"] ["math" "\\frac{x-7}{-3}=z-11,\\,y=-9"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083592/state.json" "file_sha256" "79b531f4a33a8e6ecdac0b273aeb259d8b4cc6381e4caa988d49014db4c079a0"} {"question" "q-71039" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{x-7}{-3}=\\frac{y+9}{2},\\,z=11"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083592/state.json" "file_sha256" "79b531f4a33a8e6ecdac0b273aeb259d8b4cc6381e4caa988d49014db4c079a0"} {"question" "q-71035" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the Cartesian equation for the line with the following vector equation:\n\n$$\nr=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 10 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083592/state.json" "file_sha256" "79b531f4a33a8e6ecdac0b273aeb259d8b4cc6381e4caa988d49014db4c079a0"} {"question" "q-71035" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Using the usual formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-(-3)}{0}=\\frac{y-6}{10}=\\frac{z-(-2)}{3}=t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\n$$\n\nor equivalently\n\n$$\n\\frac{x+3}{0}=\\frac{y-6}{10}=\\frac{z+2}{3},\n$$\n\nwhich is problematic because $\\frac{x+3}{0}$ involves division by zero.\n\nHowever, notice that in our line, the $x$ component always maintains the constant value of $x=-3:$\n\n$$\nr=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 6+10t \\\\ -2+3t\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\n$$\n\nSo, in the Cartesian equation, we remove the $\\frac{x+3}{0}$ part and instead write $x=-3$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{y-6}{10}=\\frac{z+2}{3},\\,x=-3\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083592/state.json" "file_sha256" "79b531f4a33a8e6ecdac0b273aeb259d8b4cc6381e4caa988d49014db4c079a0"} {"question" "q-71035" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083592/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f459ebae02c303ffbdaf1e7e6d5d50f6fa0548822275602509ac63b3920a721f"} {"question" "q-71035" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083592/state.json" "file_sha256" "79b531f4a33a8e6ecdac0b273aeb259d8b4cc6381e4caa988d49014db4c079a0"} {"question" "q-71035" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{x}{-3}=\\frac{y+10}{6}=\\frac{z+3}{-2}"] ["math" "\\frac{x}{-3}=\\frac{y-10}{6}=\\frac{z-3}{-2}"] ["math" "\\frac{y+6}{10}=\\frac{z-2}{3},\\,x=3"] ["math" "\\frac{y-6}{10}=\\frac{z+2}{3},\\,x=-3"] ["text" "None of the listed"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083592/state.json" "file_sha256" "79b531f4a33a8e6ecdac0b273aeb259d8b4cc6381e4caa988d49014db4c079a0"} {"question" "q-71035" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{y-6}{10}=\\frac{z+2}{3},\\,x=-3"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083592/state.json" "file_sha256" "79b531f4a33a8e6ecdac0b273aeb259d8b4cc6381e4caa988d49014db4c079a0"} {"question" "q-127412" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the Cartesian equation for the line with the following vector equation:\n\n$$\nr=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083592/state.json" "file_sha256" "79b531f4a33a8e6ecdac0b273aeb259d8b4cc6381e4caa988d49014db4c079a0"} {"question" "q-127412" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Using the usual formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-(-3)}{3}=\\frac{y-2}{0}=\\frac{z-1}{-2}=t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\n$$\n\nor equivalently\n\n$$\n\\frac{x+3}{3}=\\frac{y-2}{0}=\\frac{z-1}{-2},\n$$\n\nwhich is problematic because $\\frac{y-2}{0}$ involves division by zero.\n\nHowever, notice that in our line, the $y$ component always maintains the constant value of $y=2:$\n\n$$\nr=[\\begin{aligned}-3+3t \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1-2t\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\n$$\n\nSo, in the Cartesian equation, we remove the $\\frac{y-2}{0}$ part and instead write $y=2,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x+3}{3}=\\frac{z-1}{-2},\\,y=2\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083592/state.json" "file_sha256" "79b531f4a33a8e6ecdac0b273aeb259d8b4cc6381e4caa988d49014db4c079a0"} {"question" "q-127412" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083592/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f459ebae02c303ffbdaf1e7e6d5d50f6fa0548822275602509ac63b3920a721f"} {"question" "q-127412" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083592/state.json" "file_sha256" "79b531f4a33a8e6ecdac0b273aeb259d8b4cc6381e4caa988d49014db4c079a0"} {"question" "q-127412" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{x+3}{-3}=\\frac{z-1}{2},\\,y=2"] ["math" "\\frac{x+3}{3}=\\frac{z+1}{-2},\\,y=-2"] ["math" "\\frac{x+3}{3}=\\frac{z-1}{-2},\\,y=2"] ["math" "\\frac{x-3}{3}=\\frac{z+1}{-2},\\,y=2"] ["math" "\\frac{x-3}{3}=\\frac{z-1}{-2},\\,y=-2"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083592/state.json" "file_sha256" "79b531f4a33a8e6ecdac0b273aeb259d8b4cc6381e4caa988d49014db4c079a0"} {"question" "q-127412" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{x+3}{3}=\\frac{z-1}{-2},\\,y=2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083592/state.json" "file_sha256" "79b531f4a33a8e6ecdac0b273aeb259d8b4cc6381e4caa988d49014db4c079a0"} {"question" "q-71048" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the Cartesian equation for the line with the following vector equation:\n\n$$\nr=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083592/state.json" "file_sha256" "79b531f4a33a8e6ecdac0b273aeb259d8b4cc6381e4caa988d49014db4c079a0"} {"question" "q-71048" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Using the usual formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-3}{7}=\\frac{y-(-1)}{-3}=\\frac{z-6}{0}=t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\n$$\n\nor equivalently\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-3}{7}=\\frac{y+1}{-3}=\\frac{z-6}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is problematic because $\\frac{z-6}{0}$ involves division by zero.\n\nHowever, notice that in our line, the $z$ component always maintains the constant value of $z=6:$\n\n$$\nr=[\\begin{aligned}3+7t \\\\ -1-3t \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\n$$\n\nSo, in the Cartesian equation, we remove the $\\frac{z-6}{0}$ part and instead write $z=6$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-3}{7}=\\frac{y+1}{-3},\\,z=6\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083592/state.json" "file_sha256" "79b531f4a33a8e6ecdac0b273aeb259d8b4cc6381e4caa988d49014db4c079a0"} {"question" "q-71048" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083592/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f459ebae02c303ffbdaf1e7e6d5d50f6fa0548822275602509ac63b3920a721f"} {"question" "q-71048" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083592/state.json" "file_sha256" "79b531f4a33a8e6ecdac0b273aeb259d8b4cc6381e4caa988d49014db4c079a0"} {"question" "q-71048" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{x+3}{7}=\\frac{y-1}{-3},\\,z=-6"] ["math" "\\frac{x+7}{3}=\\frac{y-3}{-1},\\,z=0"] ["math" "\\frac{x-3}{7}=\\frac{y+1}{-3},\\,z=6"] ["math" "\\frac{x-3}{7}=\\frac{y+1}{-3}=z-6"] ["math" "\\frac{x-7}{3}=\\frac{y+3}{-1},\\,z=0"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083592/state.json" "file_sha256" "79b531f4a33a8e6ecdac0b273aeb259d8b4cc6381e4caa988d49014db4c079a0"} {"question" "q-71048" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{x-3}{7}=\\frac{y+1}{-3},\\,z=6"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083592/state.json" "file_sha256" "79b531f4a33a8e6ecdac0b273aeb259d8b4cc6381e4caa988d49014db4c079a0"} {"question" "q-127461" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the Cartesian equation for the line with the following vector equation:\n\n$$\nr=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083592/state.json" "file_sha256" "79b531f4a33a8e6ecdac0b273aeb259d8b4cc6381e4caa988d49014db4c079a0"} {"question" "q-127461" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Using the usual formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-3}{-5}=\\frac{y-0}{1}=\\frac{z-(-2)}{0}=t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\n$$\n\nor equivalently\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-3}{-5}=\\frac{y}{1}=\\frac{z+2}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is problematic because $\\frac{z+2}{0}$ involves division by zero.\n\nHowever, notice that in our line, the $z$ component always maintains the constant value of $z=-2:$\n\n$$\nr=[\\begin{aligned}3-5t \\\\ t \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\n$$\n\nSo, in the Cartesian equation, we remove the $\\frac{z+2}{0}$ part and instead write $z=-2,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-3}{-5}=\\frac{y}{1},\\,z=-2\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083592/state.json" "file_sha256" "79b531f4a33a8e6ecdac0b273aeb259d8b4cc6381e4caa988d49014db4c079a0"} {"question" "q-127461" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083592/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f459ebae02c303ffbdaf1e7e6d5d50f6fa0548822275602509ac63b3920a721f"} {"question" "q-127461" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083592/state.json" "file_sha256" "79b531f4a33a8e6ecdac0b273aeb259d8b4cc6381e4caa988d49014db4c079a0"} {"question" "q-127461" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{x+3}{-5}=\\frac{y}{1},\\,z=2"] ["math" "\\frac{x-3}{-5}=\\frac{y}{1},\\,z=-2"] ["math" "\\frac{x-3}{5}=\\frac{y}{-1},\\,z=-2"] ["math" "\\frac{x-3}{5}=\\frac{y}{2},\\,z=2"] ["math" "\\frac{x-5}{-3}=\\frac{y}{1},\\,z=-2"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083592/state.json" "file_sha256" "79b531f4a33a8e6ecdac0b273aeb259d8b4cc6381e4caa988d49014db4c079a0"} {"question" "q-127461" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{x-3}{-5}=\\frac{y}{1},\\,z=-2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083592/state.json" "file_sha256" "79b531f4a33a8e6ecdac0b273aeb259d8b4cc6381e4caa988d49014db4c079a0"} {"question" "q-32829" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083592/assets/58076d96d149ff4521103fd15c7187bc1a58595ab20f28be92be0f79385aee61.png)\n\nFind a Cartesian equation of the line shown in the figure above." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083592/state.json" "file_sha256" "79b531f4a33a8e6ecdac0b273aeb259d8b4cc6381e4caa988d49014db4c079a0"} {"question" "q-32829" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We know that the line passes through the points $P(-1,1,4)$ and $Q(3,-2,2).$ Now, we need to find a vector parallel to the given line. Let's consider the vector $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ which is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =⟨3,-2,2⟩-⟨-1,1,4⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4,-3,-2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the point $P$ and the vector $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$, we have that the Cartesian (canonical) equation of the given line is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-(-1)}{4}=\\frac{y-1}{-3}=\\frac{z-4}{-2}=t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\n$$\n\nor equivalently\n\n$$\n\\frac{x+1}{4}=\\frac{y-1}{-3}=\\frac{z-4}{-2}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083592/state.json" "file_sha256" "79b531f4a33a8e6ecdac0b273aeb259d8b4cc6381e4caa988d49014db4c079a0"} {"question" "q-32829" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083592/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f459ebae02c303ffbdaf1e7e6d5d50f6fa0548822275602509ac63b3920a721f"} {"question" "q-32829" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083592/state.json" "file_sha256" "79b531f4a33a8e6ecdac0b273aeb259d8b4cc6381e4caa988d49014db4c079a0"} {"question" "q-32829" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{x+1}{4}=\\frac{y-1}{-3}=\\frac{z-4}{-2}"] ["math" "\\frac{x+4}{-1}=\\frac{y-3}{1}=\\frac{z-2}{4}"] ["math" "\\frac{x-1}{4}=\\frac{y+1}{-3}=\\frac{z+4}{-2}"] ["math" "\\frac{x-4}{-1}=\\frac{y+3}{1}=\\frac{z+2}{4}"] ["math" "\\frac{x}{4}=\\frac{y}{-3}=\\frac{z}{-2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083592/state.json" "file_sha256" "79b531f4a33a8e6ecdac0b273aeb259d8b4cc6381e4caa988d49014db4c079a0"} {"question" "q-32829" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{x+1}{4}=\\frac{y-1}{-3}=\\frac{z-4}{-2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083592/state.json" "file_sha256" "79b531f4a33a8e6ecdac0b273aeb259d8b4cc6381e4caa988d49014db4c079a0"} {"question" "q-71030" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find a Cartesian equation of the straight line that passes through $P(9,3,-7)$ and is parallel to $v=⟨4,-5,3⟩.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083592/state.json" "file_sha256" "79b531f4a33a8e6ecdac0b273aeb259d8b4cc6381e4caa988d49014db4c079a0"} {"question" "q-71030" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Remember that the Cartesian (canonical) equation of the straight line that passes through the point $P({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ and is parallel to the vector $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y},{v}_{z}⟩$, is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}}=\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}}=\\frac{z-{z}_{0}}{{v}_{z}}=t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-9}{4}=\\frac{y-3}{-5}=\\frac{z-(-7)}{3}=t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\n$$\n\nor equivalently\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-9}{4}=\\frac{y-3}{-5}=\\frac{z+7}{3}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083592/state.json" "file_sha256" "79b531f4a33a8e6ecdac0b273aeb259d8b4cc6381e4caa988d49014db4c079a0"} {"question" "q-71030" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083592/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f459ebae02c303ffbdaf1e7e6d5d50f6fa0548822275602509ac63b3920a721f"} {"question" "q-71030" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083592/state.json" "file_sha256" "79b531f4a33a8e6ecdac0b273aeb259d8b4cc6381e4caa988d49014db4c079a0"} {"question" "q-71030" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{x+4}{9}=\\frac{y-5}{3}=\\frac{z+3}{-7}"] ["math" "\\frac{x+9}{4}=\\frac{y+3}{-5}=\\frac{z-7}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{x-4}{9}=\\frac{y+5}{3}=\\frac{z-3}{-7}"] ["math" "\\frac{x-9}{4}=\\frac{y-3}{-5}=\\frac{z+7}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{x}{4}=\\frac{y}{-5}=\\frac{z}{3}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083592/state.json" "file_sha256" "79b531f4a33a8e6ecdac0b273aeb259d8b4cc6381e4caa988d49014db4c079a0"} {"question" "q-71030" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{x-9}{4}=\\frac{y-3}{-5}=\\frac{z+7}{3}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083592/state.json" "file_sha256" "79b531f4a33a8e6ecdac0b273aeb259d8b4cc6381e4caa988d49014db4c079a0"} {"question" "q-33234" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083592/assets/1f9599192c1979f40fc519458019ed3296d9a5ef9048aaed41aab973c62e0be8.png)\n\nFind a Cartesian equation of the line shown in the figure above." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083592/state.json" "file_sha256" "79b531f4a33a8e6ecdac0b273aeb259d8b4cc6381e4caa988d49014db4c079a0"} {"question" "q-33234" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We know that the line passes through the points $P(-2,3,1)$ and $Q(5,-1,4).$ Now, we need to find a vector parallel to the given line. Let's consider the vector $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ which is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =⟨5,-1,4⟩-⟨-2,3,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨7,-4,3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the point $P$ and the vector $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$, we have that the Cartesian (canonical) equation of the given line is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-(-2)}{7}=\\frac{y-3}{-4}=\\frac{z-1}{3}=t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\n$$\n\nor equivalently\n\n$$\n\\frac{x+2}{7}=\\frac{y-3}{-4}=\\frac{z-1}{3}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083592/state.json" "file_sha256" "79b531f4a33a8e6ecdac0b273aeb259d8b4cc6381e4caa988d49014db4c079a0"} {"question" "q-33234" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083592/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f459ebae02c303ffbdaf1e7e6d5d50f6fa0548822275602509ac63b3920a721f"} {"question" "q-33234" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083592/state.json" "file_sha256" "79b531f4a33a8e6ecdac0b273aeb259d8b4cc6381e4caa988d49014db4c079a0"} {"question" "q-33234" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{x+2}{2}=\\frac{y-3}{3}=\\frac{z-1}{1}"] ["math" "\\frac{x+2}{7}=\\frac{y-3}{-4}=\\frac{z-1}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{x+7}{2}=\\frac{y+4}{3},\\,z=3"] ["math" "\\frac{x+7}{2}=\\frac{y+4}{3}=\\frac{z-3}{1}"] ["math" "\\frac{x}{7}=\\frac{y}{-4}=\\frac{z}{3}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083592/state.json" "file_sha256" "79b531f4a33a8e6ecdac0b273aeb259d8b4cc6381e4caa988d49014db4c079a0"} {"question" "q-33234" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{x+2}{7}=\\frac{y-3}{-4}=\\frac{z-1}{3}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083592/state.json" "file_sha256" "79b531f4a33a8e6ecdac0b273aeb259d8b4cc6381e4caa988d49014db4c079a0"} {"question" "q-127527" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083592/assets/8722b93655e069e2f35147b572c16ed7af408a86f8a88597c79aea8fe59e9c4d.png)\n\nFind a Cartesian equation of the line shown in the figure above." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083592/state.json" "file_sha256" "79b531f4a33a8e6ecdac0b273aeb259d8b4cc6381e4caa988d49014db4c079a0"} {"question" "q-127527" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We know that the line passes through the points $P(2,-1,3)$ and $Q(4,-3,3).$ Now, we need to find a vector parallel to the given line. Let's consider the vector $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ which is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =⟨4,-3,3⟩-⟨2,-1,3⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,-2,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the point $P$ and the vector $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$, we have that the Cartesian (canonical) equation of the given line is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-2}{2}=\\frac{y-(-1)}{-2}=\\frac{z-3}{0}=t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\n$$\n\nor equivalently\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-2}{2}=\\frac{y+1}{-2}=\\frac{z-3}{0}.\n$$\n\nBut the above equation is problematic because $\\frac{z-3}{0}$ involves division by zero.\n\nHowever, in our line, the $z$ component always maintains the constant value of $z=3.$ For example, our two points $P(2,-1,3)$ and $Q(4,-3,3)$ both have $z$-coordinates of $z=3.$\n\nSo, in the Cartesian equation, we remove the $\\frac{z-3}{0}$ part and instead write $z=3,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-2}{2}=\\frac{y+1}{-2},\\,z=3.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083592/state.json" "file_sha256" "79b531f4a33a8e6ecdac0b273aeb259d8b4cc6381e4caa988d49014db4c079a0"} {"question" "q-127527" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083592/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f459ebae02c303ffbdaf1e7e6d5d50f6fa0548822275602509ac63b3920a721f"} {"question" "q-127527" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083592/state.json" "file_sha256" "79b531f4a33a8e6ecdac0b273aeb259d8b4cc6381e4caa988d49014db4c079a0"} {"question" "q-127527" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{x+2}{2}=\\frac{y+1}{4},\\,z=3"] ["math" "\\frac{x-2}{-2}=\\frac{y+1}{-4},\\,z=-3"] ["math" "\\frac{x-2}{-2}=\\frac{y+1}{4},\\,z=3"] ["math" "\\frac{x-2}{2}=\\frac{y+1}{-2},\\,z=3"] ["math" "\\frac{x-2}{2}=\\frac{y-1}{-4},\\,z=-3"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083592/state.json" "file_sha256" "79b531f4a33a8e6ecdac0b273aeb259d8b4cc6381e4caa988d49014db4c079a0"} {"question" "q-127527" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{x-2}{2}=\\frac{y+1}{-2},\\,z=3"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083592/state.json" "file_sha256" "79b531f4a33a8e6ecdac0b273aeb259d8b4cc6381e4caa988d49014db4c079a0"} {"question" "q-127466" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083592/assets/1647eb5cca32c6fdc99e23507db151a8299b29bfa70ebfbaa941e17fe4638586.png)\n\nFind a Cartesian equation of the line shown in the figure above." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083592/state.json" "file_sha256" "79b531f4a33a8e6ecdac0b273aeb259d8b4cc6381e4caa988d49014db4c079a0"} {"question" "q-127466" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We know that the line passes through the points $P(-1,4,5)$ and $Q(7,-1,2).$ Now, we need to find a vector parallel to the given line. Let's consider the vector $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ which is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =⟨7,-1,2⟩-⟨-1,4,5⟩ \\\\  & =⟨8,-5,-3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the point $P$ and the vector $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$, we have that the Cartesian (canonical) equation of the given line is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-(-1)}{8}=\\frac{y-4}{-5}=\\frac{z-5}{-3}=t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\n$$\n\nor equivalently\n\n$$\n\\frac{x+1}{8}=\\frac{y-4}{-5}=\\frac{z-5}{-3}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083592/state.json" "file_sha256" "79b531f4a33a8e6ecdac0b273aeb259d8b4cc6381e4caa988d49014db4c079a0"} {"question" "q-127466" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083592/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f459ebae02c303ffbdaf1e7e6d5d50f6fa0548822275602509ac63b3920a721f"} {"question" "q-127466" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083592/state.json" "file_sha256" "79b531f4a33a8e6ecdac0b273aeb259d8b4cc6381e4caa988d49014db4c079a0"} {"question" "q-127466" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{x+1}{-8}=\\frac{y-4}{-3}=\\frac{z-5}{-5}"] ["math" "\\frac{x+1}{-8}=\\frac{y-4}{-5}=\\frac{z-5}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{x+1}{8}=\\frac{y+4}{-5}=\\frac{z+5}{-3}"] ["math" "\\frac{x+1}{8}=\\frac{y-4}{-5}=\\frac{z-5}{-3}"] ["math" "\\frac{x-1}{8}=\\frac{y-4}{5}=\\frac{z+5}{-3}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083592/state.json" "file_sha256" "79b531f4a33a8e6ecdac0b273aeb259d8b4cc6381e4caa988d49014db4c079a0"} {"question" "q-127466" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{x+1}{8}=\\frac{y-4}{-5}=\\frac{z-5}{-3}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083592/state.json" "file_sha256" "79b531f4a33a8e6ecdac0b273aeb259d8b4cc6381e4caa988d49014db4c079a0"} {"question" "e-4910" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the straight line with the canonical equation $\\dfrac{x}{5}=\\dfrac{y+3}{3}=\\dfrac{z-2}{1}.$ Find the vector equation of the line." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083592/example-4910.json" "file_sha256" "d00716c854f37c3ddb43f4b5c57c0a72608e5d8f3ac0baf88f436f53c86858be"} {"question" "e-4910" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Remember that the Cartesian (canonical) equation of the straight line that passes through the point $P(x_0,y_0,z_0)$ and is parallel to the vector $\\mathbf{v}=\\langle v_x, v_y, v_z \\rangle$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x-x_0}{v_x}=\\dfrac{y-y_0}{v_y}=\\dfrac{z-z_0}{v_z} = t, \\qquad t \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).\n$$\n\nComparing the above to our given equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x}{5}=\\dfrac{y-(-3)}{3}=\\dfrac{z-2}{1},\n$$\n\nwe find that our line passes through the point $P(0,-3,2)$ and is parallel to $\\mathbf{v}=\\langle 5,3,1 \\rangle.$ Therefore, its vector equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =p+tv \\\\ r & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083592/example-4910.json" "file_sha256" "d00716c854f37c3ddb43f4b5c57c0a72608e5d8f3ac0baf88f436f53c86858be"} {"question" "e-4911" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the straight line with the parametric equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=1-2t \\\\ y=4t \\\\ z=-2+3t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor $t \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).$ Find the Cartesian equation of this line." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083592/example-4911.json" "file_sha256" "710c6d944559a0b742bf354baa7d697be7e19f102e9d7aed6c9795d00d558051"} {"question" "e-4911" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's find the corresponding vector equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1-2t \\\\ 4t \\\\ -2+3t\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-2t \\\\ 4t \\\\ 3t\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ r & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).$\n\nThis implies that the given line passes through the point $P(1,0,-2)$ and is parallel to the vector $\\mathbf{v}=\\langle -2, 4, 3 \\rangle.$ So, its Cartesian (canonical) equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x-1}{-2}=\\dfrac{y}{4}=\\dfrac{z-(-2)}{3} = t, \t\\qquad t \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).\n$$\n\nSimplifying, we reach\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x-1}{-2}=\\dfrac{y}{4}=\\dfrac{z+2}{3}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083592/example-4911.json" "file_sha256" "710c6d944559a0b742bf354baa7d697be7e19f102e9d7aed6c9795d00d558051"} {"question" "e-5021" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the Cartesian equation for the line with the following vector equation:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083592/assets/4b3aa22ba1342c45d422c227258837ae080ee705f2580437c775bd2beaf8afeb.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083592/example-5021.json" "file_sha256" "047ae5968f5f39eda11dcd5e2788d659ba9c1a76ec001dff86d78783beee2205"} {"question" "e-5021" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Using the usual formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x-1}{4}=\\dfrac{y-2}{0}=\\dfrac{z-3}{5} = t,\t\\qquad t \\in (-\\infty,\\infty),\n$$\n\nor equivalently\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x-1}{4}=\\dfrac{y-2}{0}=\\dfrac{z-3}{5},\n$$\n\nwhich is problematic because $\\dfrac{y-2}{0}$ involves division by zero.\n\nHowever, notice that in our line, the $y$ component always maintains the constant value of $y=2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083592/assets/cafe7ac2b6224a50a288cabf6b6838c6a85a722404c9ebd20238f689f196b841.png)\n\nSo, in the Cartesian equation, we remove the $\\dfrac{y-2}{0}$ part and instead write $y=2,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x-1}{4}=\\dfrac{z-3}{5}, \\; y=2\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083592/example-5021.json" "file_sha256" "047ae5968f5f39eda11dcd5e2788d659ba9c1a76ec001dff86d78783beee2205"} {"question" "e-4912" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find a Cartesian equation of the line shown in the figure below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083592/assets/792bf09ff2546903b863a879a2518c42bbd30fdfce652bd4e22b1ad19aefc7c9.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083592/example-4912.json" "file_sha256" "e6d02c788d664f2969bfdae358ff74bb54351e5afa80ef80ab29cbada409a5c7"} {"question" "e-4912" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We know that the line passes through the points ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083592/assets/8f40930bbc65b25e23296d4a6fae2bb12da4713b1b65a0444ed6aedb8287f857.png) and ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083592/assets/10c3f2aff07e3e42290e5fddc4751c493a0ca3839907c32a627bcbdbcc6c8aa8.png) Now, we ne